A larger Moth website is a tree of pages and shared code. Each page has an
`@entry.moth` file. Reusable navigation and layout helpers can live in a
scoped support package.

### One site, several pages

[codeblock, $code "text":
    moth-site/
      config.moth
      @entry.moth
      components/
        @package.moth
        navigation.moth
        sidebar.moth
        sidebar.css
      docs/
        @entry.moth
        functions/
          @entry.moth
          introduction.mtf
]

Each directory containing `@entry.moth` produces a page. The HTML builder
derives its route from the directory. `docs/functions/@entry.moth` produces
`docs/functions/index.html`.

The `components/` directory publishes `@components` to its permitted
consumers. Page files import its public declarations without copying source
or running the package as a page.

### The Functions page

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- docs/functions/@entry.moth

    @components navbar, sidebar
    @introduction content as introduction

    [$in title:Functions]
    [$in description:Functions, parameters and returns.]

    [navbar]
    [sidebar]

    [$md:
        # Functions

        Functions declare their parameters and results.
    ]

    [introduction]
]

The page mixes authored Markdown with imported templates. A `.mtf` asset
supplies a compile-time `content` value, so a longer chapter can remain in
`introduction.mtf` rather than making the entry file unwieldy.

Direct entry templates are **constant by default**. A direct `~[...]`
fragment explicitly requests runtime evaluation. Both are different from a
named template stored in a binding.

### Build a public helper surface

The support package facade exposes only what pages should use:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/@package.moth

    $export
    @navigation navbar

    $export
    @sidebar sidebar
]

Each `$export` publishes the selected declaration. Private helpers remain
inside the component package.

Start with this shape. Add a new module only when it represents a useful
visibility or ownership boundary, not for every template or stylesheet.
